Remark 3. Everycontinuousfunctionorpiece-wisecontinuousfunction(definedfromR \u2192 R)aremeasurable
functions.
Theorem 2. Let X be a random variable defined over (\u2126,F,P). Suppose f : R \u2192 R is a measurable function
(with respect to \u03c3-fields BR and BR). Then the distribution of random variable Y = f(X) is fully determined
in terms of distribution of X.
Proof. We have
F (y) = P(Y \u2264 y)
= P(f(X) \u2264 y)
= P(X \u2208 f\u22121(\u2212\u221e,y])
Since f is a measurable function and X is well defined, the CDF of Y is also well defined.
